## Speculative deletion — evaluate whether this is worth doing (implementation is explicitly NOT the todo)
`rapid-feedback.user.js:1056–1059` — `load()` falls back to the pre-rename key `rapid_feedback_annotations` and copies its payload into the current `STORAGE_KEY`. Three lines, dormant since the v2-era rename (the current envelope is versioned; the pre-envelope array shape is handled separately at line 1065).

## GOES / STAYS / BREAKS
- **GOES (if evaluated worthwhile):** the 4-line legacy-key fallback block.
- **STAYS:** the versioned envelope, corrupt-row skip, and pre-envelope array handling — all current-format logic, all tested.
- **BREAKS (the risk to weigh):** on any origin the author has not visited since the rename, old annotations would remain in localStorage but silently never load. No test covers the migration path (rg: the legacy key name appears exactly once in the repo). The flip side: the migration has already run on every origin the author actually uses, so the residual value may be zero.

## Evidence
- `rapid-feedback.user.js:1053–1073` (`load()`), legacy key referenced once repo-wide.
- No harness coverage: `rg "rapid_feedback_annotations" test/` → nothing.

## Decision question
Is this migration path worth carrying for a personal tool whose author is the primary user? Options: (a) delete it, (b) keep it and add a one-line comment dating the decision, (c)
=== #35 ===
[wave 2026-09-12] Evaluate: harness interaction helpers — waitFor predicates, popover action/comment/submit, state snapshot | OPEN | []
Wave 2026-09-12 0700, abstraction-zoom F3+F4+F5 (MEDIUM, MEDIUM, LOW). **Evaluate whether worth doing.**

**Evidence:** hand-rolled `setTimeout` settling at ~50 sites (representative: run.js:17,55,72,79,86,102...; run6.js:11-14,47,61,75-79...; full list in report F3). Popover DOM mechanics (find-by-text/index, set value, dispatch input, click, settle) re-encoded at 30+ sites (F4: run.js:37-44,50-54,67-71...; run5.js:60-65,73-86,104-114...). Ad-hoc state snapshots (open/rows/comment/title/lost) re-mapped per suite (F5).

**Proposed bricks:** `waitForHarness(page, condition, { timeoutMs, description })` + focused predicates (`waitForPopover`, `waitForAnnotationCount`) — replaces guessed sleeps with bounded polling; `setPopoverComment`, `clickPopoverAction(page, labelOrMatcher)`, `submitPopover({ comment, tab, styleInput })`; `readPopover(page, fields)` snapshot. Keep raw `delay(ms)` only for genuinely time-based behavior (e.g. run6's CDP slow-release).

**Honest counterpoint (from the same report):** scenario-specific snapshots are sometimes clearer than a universal state object, and stable duplicated setup across independently-failing suites is not automatically a defect (forced-subtraction concurs). Highest-value slice if adopted: waitFor predicates — they also shrink flake diagnosis. Sequenced after #1 like the other harness work.

